**Search and Rejection Summary**
- I screened roughly 30 documents across Egyptian official pages, UN press materials, Reuters mirrors, and regional reporting.
- I kept 12 new source-claim units because they add distinct value: Gaza mediation, Ukraine diplomacy, Libya political-track hosting, Sudan support, and the GERD dispute.
- I rejected or deprioritized duplicate coverage of the same Gaza ceasefire or Ukraine statements when the later publisher only repeated the same claim without adding a new factual layer.
- I rejected broad commentary and background explainers that did not add a ruler-attributable 2022 action.
- I rejected out-of-period material unless it was only being used as background; for this chapter I kept the focus on 2022.
- I hit a few direct-site fetch failures on SIS and UN pages, so I relied on the search snippets and mirrored reporting where the direct page was unavailable.

**Remaining Gaps**
- There is still no direct 2022 evidence of Sisi ordering or supervising a military operation outside domestic security, so 2B.2, 2B.4, and 2B.5 remain mostly inferential or weak.
- The strongest evidence is diplomatic and mediation-oriented; it shows restraint, but not necessarily deep transformation of regional security.
- The record is thin on the internal decision chain, so it is hard to separate Sisi personally from the broader Egyptian security apparatus in some Libya, Sudan, and Gaza actions.
- More search would help only if you want tighter corroboration from non-Egyptian sources on the GERD and Libya tracks, or more direct evidence on any offensive military role.

